== Children Getting Parents’ Permission

=== §. 11. #emph[Of children’s obedience.]

The obedience of children most proves the authority of parents, and is the surest evidence of the honor a child gives his parent. That is why it is #footnote[Eph 6:1. Col 3:20.] expressly named in the text, and all the other duties are included under it. Reverence without obedience is a mere mockery, not at all acceptable. Of the two, a child would do better to fail in the former, as the #footnote[Matt 21:31.] parable of the two sons shows. Reverence, compared with obedience, is only an outward honor of courtesy. Obedience is a true, real honor, the surest test of a dutiful child. Obedience is a duty so much children’s own that the apostle applies it to them as their own attribute, saying, “As obedient children, do not conform,”#footnote[1 Pet 1:14.] etc. The example of Christ is set before us in this as a pattern: of his parents it is said that he “was obedient to them.”#footnote[Luke 2:51.] Solomon counts the neglect of it a #emph[despising]#footnote[Prov 23:22.] of a parent.

Contrary to it are disobedience and rebellion, the greatest blow #footnote[Disobedience most contrary to parents’ authority.] to parents’ authority there can be. For to what end is authority over those who resist it and rebel against it? The #footnote[2 Tim 3:2.] apostle counts disobedient children among the most wicked people there are, and he describes their disobedience by #footnote[Titus 1:6 τέκνα ἀνυπότακτα.] a metaphor taken from untamed, headstrong beasts that will not be brought under the yoke. So the word is not unfitly translated #emph[unruly.] And it corresponds in some measure to a #footnote[Deut 13:13. בני־בליעל #emph[ex non & profuit. Hier. absque iugo. quasi] בלי עול] Hebrew phrase given to disobedient children, namely #emph[sons of Belial.] By its derivation this means #emph[sons without profit;] or, as some will have it, #emph[sons without yoke:] that is, children who, refusing to be in subjection to their parents, are of no profit in any way, but do much harm and cause great grief. The #footnote[Deut 21:18, #emph[etc.]] punishment that God’s law appointed for disobedient and rebellious children was a public, shameful death.

=== §. 12. #emph[Of children’s forbearing to do things without their parents’ consent.]

So that children may know their duty in this respect the better, #footnote[The parts of a child’s obedience.] I will set out distinctly both the #emph[parts] and the #emph[extent] of a child’s obedience:

+ What it consists in.
+ How far it extends.

The general #emph[parts] it consists in are two:

1\. A #emph[forbearance] from doing things without their parents’ consent. #footnote[Of the separate ways in which consent may be given, see Treatise 3, §. 19.]

2\. A #emph[performance] of such things as their parents want done.

The former of these is a duty children are most bound to while they are under their parents’ government. For during that time their parents’ consent is not only fitting but necessary,#footnote[Num 30:16.] and that for these reasons.

1\. Children are like their parents’ goods, wholly in their power, to be ordered and disposed of by them. On this ground Satan, having all that Job had put into his hand, took liberty over #footnote[Job 1:12, 19.] his children as well as over his goods and possessions.

2\. Children, while they are under government (even the eldest, who are heirs), are no different from servants. #footnote[Gal 4:1.]

3\. By God’s law given to the Jews, parents had power to sell #footnote[Exod 21:7.] their children.

4\. Parents had power to annul what their children had done. Take for example the case of a vow made to God,#footnote[Num 30:4.] which was one of the most inviolable things a person could do.

Contrary to this is the opinion and practice of many, who hold that parents’ consent is at most a fitting thing: that it is good, if children are willing, to have their parents’ consent; if they do not have it, it is no great matter; their contracts, or the other things they do, are as firm and good without their parents’ consent as with it. If this were so, how would the authority of a parent be any more than that of a wise, experienced friend? It is fitting and good to have such a person’s consent.

But so that the power of parents and the duty of children in this point may be seen the better, I will illustrate it in five particular cases: 1. Entering into a calling. 2. Making a marriage. 3. Disposing of goods. 4. Ordering clothing. 5. Making vows.

=== §. 13. #emph[Of parents’ consent to children’s entering into a calling.]

I. That children ought to have their parents’ consent in choosing their calling, and not place themselves as they please, is evident from the approved practice of the saints recorded in God’s word. Jacob#footnote[Gen 28:2.] was sent by his parents to Laban to be brought up under him. David#footnote[1 Sam 16:11, 19.] was appointed by his father to keep sheep; when Saul wanted to have David attend him, he sent to Jesse, David’s father, for him. Since Jesse#footnote[1 Sam 17:17.] was so concerned to send provisions to his three eldest sons, who followed Saul to the war, we may well think that they went to the war with his consent. It is noted of Jonadab#footnote[Jer 35:7.] that he appointed his sons to live in tents, that they did so accordingly, and that they are commended and rewarded for this obedience of theirs. Both #footnote[Justin. Mart. in Dial. cum Tryph. contr. Jud.] ancient and #footnote[#emph[Erasm. paraphr. in Mar.] 6. #emph[Bez annot. in Mar.] 6.] later divines gather that our Lord Jesus Christ #footnote[#emph[Basil. lib. de vit. solit. cap.] 15. #emph[Theod. Eccl. hist. l.] 3. 6. 23.] in his younger years, before he began to exercise his public ministry, occupied himself in his father’s trade, and that this was the way in which he showed his subjection to his parents. This conclusion is drawn by comparing Luke 2:51 (where his subjection is noted) with Mark 6:3 and Matt 13:55, where he is called #footnote[#emph[Muscul. in Mat.] 13. #emph[Danaeus schol. in Mar.] 6. #emph[Gualt. hom.] 46. #emph[in Mar. Muthes. in Mar.] 6. #emph[alijque. Reasons.]] “the carpenter” and “the carpenter’s son.”

Justice requires that parents should have a hand in placing their children, because they brought them into the world, and brought them up with much care, pains and expense while they were young, until they were fit for a calling.

Besides, God has laid it as a charge on parents that they should see their children well trained up. There is great reason, therefore, #footnote[1\. Justice.] that the parents’ consent be obtained in setting children out in a calling. #footnote[2\. God’s charge.]

=== §. 14. #emph[Of the unlawfulness of children’s entering religious orders without their parents’ consent.]

Contrary to this is the opinion of the papists, who say that children may #footnote[#emph[Licet filijs ingredi religionem inuitis parentibus. Bellarm. de Mon. lib.] 2. #emph[cap.] 36.] enter religious orders not only without the consent, but even against the mind and approval, of their parents. By this they not only defend open disobedience in children, against the express word of God, but also disable children from helping their parents in case of need. For both of these Christ rebuked the scribes and Pharisees in a similar case.

#emph[Objection.] The papists grant that if parents are in such need #footnote[Conditio est, vt parentes non sint in tali necessitate, vt sine filiorum auxilio viuere non possint. Tunc enim tenentur ex praecepto Dei parentibus adesse. Bellarm. loc. cit.] that they cannot live without their children’s help, their children must not forsake their parents by entering any religious order. For they are bound by the law of God to help their parents.

#emph[Answer.] 1. This caveat has been wrung from them by the evidence of arguments taken from God’s word and pressed on them by their opponents.

2\. It does not touch the principal argument, taken from God’s precept, which they make of no effect by this tradition of theirs.

3\. Though parents are not in such extreme need at the time when their children first enter their religious order, yet they may be afterward. But once children have entered, the papists hold it utterly unlawful for them to attend on their parents to help them, whatever the parents’ need.

#emph[Objection.] Children who have entered religious orders may #footnote[Eos iuvant quomodo religiosos decet, nimirum precibus apud Deum. Bellarm. ibid.] help their parents as befits religious persons: by their prayers to God.

#emph[Answer.] 1. This is exactly the Pharisees’ Corban, which Christ mentions in Mark 7:11, and by which he plainly exposes the hypocrisy of the Pharisees, who made a show of religion into a hindrance to the obedience God required of children.

2\. To pray for something a man does not try to do, when he is able to do it, is a plain mocking of God.

The arguments they bring to confirm their erroneous opinion are taken from extraordinary examples or from mystical resemblances, such as

+ Abraham’s leaving his father’s house. #footnote[Gen 12:1. Deut 33:9.]
+ Levi’s speech about his father and mother, when he said, “I do not consider them.”
+ The advice given to the royal queen, “Forget … your father’s #footnote[Ps 45:10.] house.”
+ The test of our love for Christ, by loving him more #footnote[Matt 10:37.] than father or mother.
+ Christ’s forbidding one who followed him to go and #footnote[Luke 9:60.] bury his father.

#emph[Answer.] 1. As for Abraham’s example: 1. It cannot be proved that he left his father’s house without his father’s consent. 2. He was then married, and so of another house. 3. He had #footnote[Gen 12:1.] an express, particular charge from God to leave his father’s house, just as he had to sacrifice his son. Unless a like charge can be shown, his example proves nothing to the purpose.

2\. As for Levi’s speech: 1. Moses noted it in connection #footnote[Exod 32:26, #emph[etc.]] with a particular zealous deed of the Levites in carrying out the vengeance of the Lord, and so it is to be counted among those extraordinary things that are not set as examples. 2. What moved the Levites to make no difference between their parents and others was the Lord’s cause. Their parents and relatives, as well as others, had notoriously sinned against God, and in that respect the Levites took no notice of them. But the parents the papists teach children to forsake are not such parents, but any parents. Now what kind of inference is this: some children have been God’s ministers in carrying out just punishment on their wicked parents; therefore children may enter such places as will exempt them from helping any parents, however well deserving? 3. The Levites had an express charge#footnote[Exod 32:27.] for what they did; but what the papists infer from their example makes the commandment of God of no effect. #footnote[Matt 15:6.]

3\. As for the #footnote[Ps 45:10.] advice to the queen: 1. It is to be taken mystically. 2. If it were taken literally, it is to be taken as given to her after marriage, when she was out of her parents’ government. 3. It concerns the #footnote[Gen 2:24.] law of marriage, and implies not an outright forsaking of parents, but a preferring of a husband before them.

4\. As for loving father and mother more than Christ: 1. It does not necessarily imply a forsaking of our parents, for we may love Christ more than them and still perform our duty to them. 2. If they are forsaken, it must be in opposition to Christ, when either Christ or they must be forsaken, because if we hold fast to them they will draw us away from Christ.

5\. As for Christ’s forbidding one who followed him to go and bury his father: 1. It was because of an extraordinary calling which that man had. 2. It is set down as a pattern for ministers, to show that they should especially attend to their own office, and leave other worldly matters to be done by those who can do them well enough. To apply it to children’s forsaking of their parents is to pervert its sense.

So we see how little to the purpose the arguments named above are brought to prove that erroneous opinion, that children may enter religious orders without their parents’ consent. I might further show how irreligious their so-called religious orders are, and so how unlawful it is to enter them even with the parents’ consent; but that is nothing to the point in hand.

=== §. 15. #emph[Of the unlawfulness of children’s traveling, and binding themselves as apprentices, without their parents’ consent.]

Contrary also to the part of children’s obedience named above is the practice of those children who travel and seek their fortunes (as they say) without their parents’ consent, like the prodigal child,#footnote[Luke 15:12, #emph[etc.]] if not worse. For it is likely that he forced a general consent from his father, since he obtained his portion of goods from him. Such children usually bring great grief to their parents, and often make them fear more than there is cause, as old Jacob feared when he did not know what had become of #footnote[Gen 37:35.] his son.

Among these failings may be counted a custom in this land, more common than lawful, for children to bind themselves as apprentices without their parents’ consent. Those who take indentures from such children, or otherwise make agreements with them without knowing that their parents consent, make themselves accessories to this fault. #footnote[Arguments to prove that children must not marry without their parents’ consent.]

=== §. 16. #emph[Of parents’ consent to the marriage of their children.]

#smallcaps[II]. That children ought to have their parents’ consent to their marriage is clear beyond all question. For

1\. God himself has given us a pattern in this. He first brought the woman to the man (Gen 2:22). By this he would show that he who gave the woman her being had a right to give her in marriage; and parents now have that right, for from them, under God, children receive their being. In this case parents stand in God’s place, and are, as it were, God’s hand to join their children in marriage.

2\. God has given express laws on this point. I pass over that general moral law, “Honor your father and mother” (which, as it is the ground of all the other duties belonging to children, is the ground of this one too). The authority and charge which God by his law (Deut 7:3) has laid on parents, to give their daughters to husbands and to take wives for their sons, has the force of a law binding children from taking wives or husbands without or against their parents’ consent. This law did not belong to the Jews only; as a branch of the moral law it is pressed on Christians, 1 Cor 7:36, 37.

To this may be added the judicial law (if it is to be counted merely judicial) of a parent’s power to give his daughter in marriage, or to refuse to give her, to the man who had deflowered her, Exod 22:17.

3\. The practice of God’s saints, recorded and approved in Scripture, has been in keeping with the law. Isaac married the wife his father provided (Gen 24:67). Jacob both obeyed his father in going to Laban’s house for a wife (Gen 28:2) and also, when he came to Laban, asked him for his daughter (Gen 29:18 etc.).

Though Samson saw a daughter of the Philistines who #footnote[#emph[Non est virginalis pudoris eligere maritum: electionem mariti parentibus deferat. Ambr. de Patr. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 9. \[It is not for a virgin’s modesty to choose a husband: let her leave the choice of a husband to her parents.\]] pleased him well, yet he would not marry her before he had his parents’ consent, Judg 14:2.

4\. These words of Tamar (2 Sam 13:13), “Speak to the king” (who was her father), “for he will not withhold me from you,” show that children were not accustomed to be married without their parents’ consent. This is further confirmed by this oath of the Israelites: “Not one of us will give his daughter #footnote[Legitimum non est coniugium nisi vxor petatur a parentibus. Euarist. in epist. ad eccles. Affric. \[It is not a lawful marriage unless the wife is sought from her parents.\]] in marriage to a Benjamite,” Judg 21:1.

5\. The#footnote[#emph[Filij sine consensu parentum ritè & iure nubere non possunt. Tertul. ad vxor. l.] 2.] ancient fathers of the church have taught children this duty in their ages, and pronounced marriages of children without their parents’ consent to be unlawful.

6\. The very #footnote[#emph[Tempore creuit amor: tedae quoque iure coissent: Sed vetuere patres. Ouid. Metam.] 4. \[In time their love grew; they would have joined by the right of the marriage torch as well, but their fathers forbade it.\] #emph[Me parenti potius quàm amori obsequi oportet. Teren. in Hecyra.] \[I ought to obey my parent rather than my love.\] Νυμφευμάτων μὲν τῶν ἐμῶν πατὴρ ἐμοῦ μέριμναν ἕξει, κοὐκ ἐμὸν τάδε κρίνειν. #emph[Eurip. in Androm.] \[My father will take care of my marriage; it is not for me to decide these things.\]] heathen have observed the justice of this. Though Shechem loved Dinah, and had deflowered her, yet he would not marry her without the consent of his father and hers, Gen 34:3 etc. Ishmael had learned as much, either from the instruction he had received in Abraham’s house or else by the light of nature; for he kept to the choice his mother made for him, Gen 21:21.

7\. Though #footnote[#emph[Concil. Trid. decr. de reform. matr. c.] 1.] the papists in other cases make the authority of parents of no effect, yet in this case they count it utterly unlawful for children to marry without or against their parents’ consent, and have made canons against it on that ground.

8\. The #footnote[#emph[Matris voluntatem in tradenda filia omnibus natura praeponit. August. epist.] 233. \[Nature sets the mother’s will before all others in giving a daughter in marriage.\]] law of nature and of nations, the civil and canon law, the common and statute law of our land: every kind of law agrees with God’s law in this point.

9\. It#footnote[#emph[Cum sponsus & sponsa benedicendi sunt a sacerdote, offerantur ecclesiae a parentibus. Grat.] 30. #emph[quaest.] 5. #emph[Cav.] \[When the bridegroom and the bride are to be blessed by the priest, let them be presented to the church by their parents.\]] has been a custom in all Christian churches throughout all ages for the parent, or someone in the parent’s #footnote[#emph[Quod ipsi Philosophi mirati sunt, seruate virgines. Ambr. de patr. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 9. \[Keep, O virgins, what the philosophers themselves admired.\]] place, to give the bride to the bridegroom at the time of the marriage; and by this the parents’ consent is openly shown.

10\. Many#footnote[#emph[Erasm. in colloq. de Matrim. Luth. in serm. de Matrim. Bucer. in Mat.] 19. #emph[Calu. Instit. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 19. §. 37. #emph[Bez. de Diuort. Zanch. de Oper. Dei par.] 3. #emph[l.] 4. #emph[c.] 1. #emph[Chemn. in Exam. part.] 2. #emph[Brent. in Catec. Melanc. loc. de coniug. Perkins in Oecon. c.] 6. alijque.] divines of good note and name have judged marriages made wholly without, or directly against, the parents’ consent (especially if the parents have just cause of objection to those marriages) to be of no force until the parent is brought to ratify them; and in many churches, after due examination of the matter, they usually count them as no marriages. Experience has shown the boldness of many children in making light of their parents’ consent in those places where marriages, once completed, are ratified #footnote[#emph[Hoc fieri debere & ciuilis & naturalis ratio suadet. Iustin. Instit. l.] 1. #emph[tit.] 10. #emph[Digest. l.] 23. #emph[tit.] 2 \[Both civil and natural reason urge that this should be done.\]] and made indissoluble, though they were made wholly without or directly against the parents’ consent. Many children think that though it is done unlawfully, yet once it is done it will stand. So if they fear they will not have their parents’ consent, they will contrive to get their marriage completed in such a way that their parents do not know of it in time to hinder it before it is done; and after it is done, they brazenly resolve to ride out, as well as they can, the storm of their parents’ displeasure. To prevent such contempt of the power of parents, and to establish the authority God has given them over their children, marriages made without or against the parents’ consent, as described above, are made void in many churches.

=== §. 17. #emph[Of the justice of the point, and reasons why children should have their parents’ consent to their marriage.]

1\. By marriage children are separated from their parents, for #footnote[The reasons given in §. 12. may be applied to this particular.] “a man will leave his father and mother and be united to his wife.”#footnote[Gen 2:24.] Is it not great reason, then, that those from whom children had their being, and by whom they have been maintained and brought up until the time of their marriage, should be told of that kind of leaving them, and give their consent to it?

2\. By the marriage of his child, a parent’s power passes over to the husband or wife of the child. And should such a power be taken away without the parent’s consent?

3\. Children, for the most part, are headstrong and rash for lack of experience, and seek more to satisfy their present carnal desire than to provide themselves a good and lasting helper. But parents, by the instinct of nature, love their children as well as children love themselves; by much experience they have a better understanding of a fitting helper; and they are better able to use their gift of discernment in this case, because it is not their own case, and yet it is the case of one whom they love as themselves, and to whom they wish as much good as to themselves. Is it not fitting, then, even for the child’s good, that in a matter of such weight as marriage the parent should have a say?

=== §. 18. #emph[Of a child’s conduct in case a parent provides an unfit mate or none at all.]

#emph[Question.] What if parents urge their children to marry someone #footnote[Whether parents may force their children to marry, or not.] they cannot feel affection for and love: must children yield obedience in this, against their mind and liking?

#emph[Answer.] If there is no just objection to the person recommended, they ought to strive with the utmost of their power #footnote[See Treatise 6, §. 54.] to bend their affection to their parents’ will. As a help to this, let them be persuaded that their parents are as concerned for their good as they themselves are, and wiser than they are. Indeed, above all they ought to pray earnestly to God (in whose hand man’s heart is, to turn it #footnote[Prov 21:1.] wherever he will) that he would be pleased to change the course of their affection and to settle it on the person their parent has chosen for them; if, at least, they see no just cause to the contrary. But if, despite all the means they can use, they still find their heart wholly averse, they may in a reverent manner entreat their parent to stop pressing that match, and to think of some other.

2\. #emph[Question.] What if the parent is negligent, and does not provide a fit match in due time: may the child not provide one for himself?

#emph[Answer.] A parent’s negligence is not a sufficient pretext for a child to cast off the subjection he owes his parent. Yet I do not deny that a child who knows where a fit match is to be had may make it known to his parent (as Samson did)#footnote[Judg 14:2.] and ask for both his consent and his help in it. And if his parent gives no ear to his humble request, he may use the mediation of his relatives or other friends. Indeed, if necessity requires that the child be married, and his parent adds willfulness to negligence and will not be moved at all, either by the humble request of his child or by the earnest pleading of any friends, an appeal may be made to the magistrate (who is in God’s place over the parent as well as over #footnote[#emph[Qui liberos iniuria prohibuerint ducere vxores vel nubere, per praesides cogantur in matrimonium collocare Digest. lib.] 23. #emph[tit.] 2. § 19. \[Those who wrongfully forbid their children to take wives or to marry are to be compelled through the governors to give them in marriage.\]] the child, and ought to give relief to the child); and what the magistrate does in that case is as good a warrant to the child as if the parent had done it.

The same means may be used if a parent is an idolater, a heretic or an atheist, and will not allow his child to be married to anyone but one of his own persuasion and disposition.

=== §. 19. #emph[Of the sin of children in marrying without their parents’ consent.]

Contrary to this is the mind and practice of those children who, thinking too lightly of their parents’ power, take matches of their own choice: sometimes secretly, without giving their parents any notice at all; and sometimes most rebelliously, against their parents’ mind and charge. They are not much unlike those in the old world who are condemned because “they took as wives whomever #footnote[Gen 6:2.] they chose” (which was one branch of the wickedness for which the world was drowned); or rather they are like Esau,#footnote[Gen 26:35.] who took such wives as turned out to be a grief to his parents. What blessing can be expected to fall on such marriages? Or rather, what curse may we not fear will follow them? God’s law is transgressed by them. His image in parents is despised. What is more the parents’ own than any goods is taken from them, either by fraud or by violence. Their souls are grieved by it, and they are #footnote[See more harms following such marriages, §. 21.] often provoked to cast off their children and curse their marriages. Now God’s curse often follows the just curse of a parent.

=== §. 20. #emph[Of objections in favor of children’s marrying without their parents’ consent, answered.]

1\. #emph[Objection.] Though Jacob married one wife according to his parents’ direction, yet he married three others (at least the two maidservants) without their consent.

1\. #emph[Answer.] Jacob’s example in marrying more wives than one is not to be justified.

2\. #emph[Answer.] Jacob had a general consent from his parents to #footnote[Gen 28:2.] take a wife from the daughters of Laban. If, therefore, his marrying two wives had been lawful, neither the one daughter nor the other would have been taken wholly without his parents’ consent. As for the two maidservants by whom he had children, neither of them was his wife: for long after they had children they are called his “maidservants,” #footnote[Gen 32:22.] and distinguished from his wives.

2\. #emph[Objection.] Servants may marry without their masters’ consent: why then may children not marry without their parents’?

1\. #emph[Answer.] #footnote[See Treatise 8, §. 17.] It is not lawful for servants to do so while the term of their covenant lasts.

2\. #emph[Answer.] Though the servitude of a servant is greater than that of a child, yet a parent has in many respects a greater power over his child than a master over his servant. The power a master has comes by a mutual covenant between him and his servant, and by the servant’s voluntary subjection to his master. But the power of a parent comes by the bond of nature, since a child has his being from his parents. Besides, this subjection of a child to his parents in the matter of marriage is not for servitude but #footnote[See the third reason in §. 17.] for the good of the child.

3\. #emph[Objection.] Children marry for themselves and not for their parents: why then should parents’ consent be insisted on so much?

1\. #emph[Answer.] Though they do not marry #emph[for] their parents, yet they marry #footnote[See the second reason in §. 17. Ps 127:3.] #emph[from] their parents: by marriage they are freed from their parents’ power.

2\. #emph[Answer.] Children are not their own. They are “a heritage from the #smallcaps[Lord]”: the Lord has given them to parents as a heritage. A child, therefore, has no more right to marry for himself without his parents’ consent than to give away his parents’ goods for himself.

=== §. 21. #emph[Of stealing children from their parents for the sake of marriage.]

Those who by fraud lure children away, or by violence carry them off, to marry them otherwise than their parents would wish, make themselves accessories to the #footnote[§. 19.] sin named above, and to its vengeance. This is a worse kind of felony than stealing a man’s goods. For children are much more truly a man’s own than his goods, and dearer to him than any goods can be; indeed, they are to be valued so much more highly, as rational creatures are to be preferred before senseless and merely sentient things. Our #footnote[#emph[Anno] 4. & 5. #emph[Phil. & Mar. stat.] 8.] statute law expressly condemns this, and imposes a severe punishment on those who offend in it. And such offenders justly deserve to be severely punished, both because of the heinousness of the sin and because of the many #footnote[Harms arising from marriages without parents’ consent.] harms that follow from it, such as #emph[alienation of parents’ affection from their children, disinheriting of heirs, enmity between the relatives of each party so married, lawsuits, ruin of families,] and (if the persons whose children are married without their parents’ consent are great and noble) #emph[disturbance of whole towns, cities and nations.] Take for example the destruction of the Shechemites, Gen 34. #footnote[#emph[Causa mali tanti coniux. Virg. Aeneid.] 6. \[A wife, the cause of so great an evil.\] #emph[Qui rapta longum cum coniuge bellum Attulit in patriam. Ouid. Met. l.] 12. \[Who, with a stolen wife, brought a long war on his country.\] #emph[lege. Hor. Carm. l.] 1. #emph[Od.] 15.] This is said to have been the cause of the ten years’ war between the Greeks and the Trojans, and of the ruin of Troy.

=== §. 22. #emph[Of ministers’ sin in marrying children without their parents’ consent.]

Those ministers also who, through #emph[carelessness,] not taking due account of the parties they marry, whether they have their parents’ consent or not, or through #emph[bribery,] being hired by a reward, marry such children as they know do not have their parents’ consent, make themselves accessories in a high degree to the #footnote[§. 19.] sin named above. Their deed is as bad as the deed of the principals themselves. Their solemnizing of such marriages emboldens both the parties so married and all the persons present at it. They greatly dishonor God’s holy ordinance, because, acting in God’s person, they say of those whom God has forbidden to be so joined together, “Those whom God has joined together, let no man separate.” If ministers did not have a hand in such unlawful marriages, they could not be made, for our church ratifies no marriage except one made by a minister. So some minister or other is guilty of this foul sin whenever any child is married without his parents’ consent. Our church therefore does well (to prevent this sin) to forbid ministers expressly to marry anyone without their parents’ consent, and to inflict a severe censure on those who offend in it.

=== §. 23. #emph[Of children’s forbearing to dispose of any of their parents’ goods without consent.]

A third branch of the subjection of children, in forbearing to do anything without their parents’ consent, concerns their parents’ #emph[goods.]

That children, though living in their parents’ house, ought not to dispose of their parents’ goods without their consent is evident from the extent of their obedience: #emph[in everything.]

Since Isaac was pleased to send Jacob to Paddan-aram without any great provision, it seems that Jacob made it a matter of conscience not to take anything secretly, and went as his father sent him, with only his staff.#footnote[Gen 32:10.] And the #footnote[Gen 31:36.] defense he made to Laban, his father-in-law, concerning things taken away shows that he held it unlawful for children secretly to carry off their parents’ goods: “What is my trespass? What is my sin?” (he says). “Have you found anything that belongs to you?”

Does he not imply by this that if Laban’s daughters had taken away any of their father’s goods, it would have been a trespass and a sin?

The apostle says of the heir (who of all the children may #footnote[#emph[Reasons.] 1. Children under parents are as servants.] seem to have the greatest right) that “as long as he is a child” (that is, under the government of his parents), “he is no different from a servant,#footnote[Gal 4:1.] although he is the owner of everything.” If he is no different from a servant, what right can he have to dispose of his parents’ goods at his pleasure? Does a servant have any such right?

It is very needful that children should be bound to their parents’ consent in this, both for the good of the parents and for the good of the children themselves.

#emph[For the parents,] so that they may know what they have #footnote[2\. Good of parents.] or do not have, and order their expenses accordingly. How can parents tell what they have, if children secretly, without their knowledge, steal and dispose of their goods?

#emph[For the children,] so that their spendthrift disposition may by this means #footnote[3\. Good of children.] be restrained (for youth is very prone to spend beyond moderation, if it has the means), and so that their parents may be the better able to “lay up” for them. #footnote[2 Cor 12:14.]

=== §. 24. #emph[Of the sin of children in stealing and squandering their parents’ goods.]

Contrary to this are both the opinion and the practice of many #footnote[It is a sin for children.] children.

As for #emph[opinion,] many think and say that whatever is their #footnote[1\. To grumble at their parents’ allowance.] parents’ is theirs too. And so, when the watchful eye and provident care of their parents restrain them from overlavish spending, or from laying out anything with their own hands, they grumble against that restraint.

Knowing and being persuaded of their subjection in this matter would be a good means to suppress that discontented disposition.

As for #emph[practice,] #footnote[2\. To steal their parents’ goods secretly. Gen 31:19. Those who counsel children to carry off their parents’ goods are accessories to theft.]

1\. Some secretly take away and steal whatever goods, money, wares or anything else of their parents’ they can come by. The Holy Spirit counts this plain theft: for when Rachel had secretly taken away her father’s idols, the Scripture says that she “stole” them.

Those who counsel and encourage children to do so make themselves accessories to this: as many busybodies and deceitful persons advise daughters, when God has taken away their mothers, to take linen and other such household goods away from their father, claiming that their father may marry another wife, who will carry everything away. And on a like pretext they also persuade sons, when their fathers die, to carry off what they can from their mother. But such pretexts are no sufficient warrant for children to deceive their parents. It would be better for children to be deprived of their parents’ goods than to enjoy them by such deceit, for they will be like that bread which “is sweet to a man, but later his #footnote[Prov 20:17.] mouth is full of gravel.”

2\. Others spend their portion in wild living, like the prodigal #footnote[3\. To spend wastefully. Luke 15:13.] child, and run into debt, and so force their parents either to pay it or to leave them to the law. Many scholars at the universities, gentlemen at the Inns of Court, and such children as are brought up somewhat liberally in their parents’ or other relatives’ houses, offend greatly in this. This excess and wild living differs little from the kind of theft named above, and ordinarily it brings as many harms as that does.

3\. Some also are so graceless and ungrateful that, when they #footnote[4\. To cheat their parents cunningly out of their goods.] have come of age and their parents have grown old, they seek to cheat their parents out of all they have, and to bring their parents under them, to be ordered by them. They labor to get possession of everything before their parents are dead, or before the parents are willing to resign any such right to their children. Such were #footnote[2 Sam 15:10. #emph[etc.]] Absalom and Adonijah. How highly displeasing such practices are to God is shown by the vengeance that fell on the heads of #footnote[1 Kgs 1:5. #emph[etc.]] those two #emph[brothers in evil,] traitorous and disloyal children.

All such children as seek after the means named above, or any other like means, to defraud their parents repay their parents’ care for them very badly, and, more like barbarians than Christians, return evil for good. They often bring poverty and disgrace on their parents and themselves. They are worse than other thieves, because they are more dearly valued and more freely trusted. Indeed, they are a very bad example to servants in the house, or to subjects in the commonwealth.

=== §. 25. #emph[Of children’s being content to be clothed according to their parents’ mind and liking.]

#smallcaps[IIII]. A fourth branch of the aforesaid subjection of children concerns their clothing: that it be no other than what agrees with their parents’ good liking. It is noted that Israel #footnote[Gen 37:3.] made Joseph a robe. Does not the particular mention of that circumstance show that parents must have the ordering of their children’s clothing? This is also implied in the reason given for Tamar’s “robe of many colors,” namely, #footnote[2 Sam 13:18.] “because this is what the king’s virgin daughters wore.” And since Rebekah had the keeping of her #footnote[Gen 27:15.] son Esau’s clothes, it appears that his clothes were to his parents’ liking; otherwise he would have hidden them from his parents. In further confirmation of this, it is noted that his clothing was pleasing to his father.

Contrary to this is the vainglorious disposition of many children, #footnote[Children’s sin in clothing themselves to the grief and disgrace of their parents.] who, to the grief and discredit of their parents, dress themselves both against the mind and above the means of their parents, and in a way unbecoming their parents’ place and calling. Among others, many ministers’ children bring much discredit on their parents by this. Let all such proud youths note how the Lord has threatened to punish even the king’s children who are dressed #footnote[Zeph 1:8.] in foreign apparel.

=== §. 26. #emph[Of children’s forbearing to bind themselves to do anything against their parents’ consent.]

V. The fifth and last branch with which I will illustrate the subjection of children named above will be the one expressly noted in the law: a child’s binding itself by a vow.

The law gives the parent power to annul his child’s #footnote[Num 30:4.] vow. It is therefore a child’s duty to abstain from vowing without his parents’ consent.

Contrary to this are such vows as the papists lure children to make, #footnote[Unlawful vows of children.] namely vows of #emph[chastity, perpetual virginity, obedience to a monastic rule, voluntary poverty,] and the like.

Even if by these vows children are not drawn to forsake their parents (which #footnote[§. 14.] we proved above to be utterly unlawful), and even if these vows were lawful in their nature (which they are not, because they are against God’s law and ordinance, and against Christian liberty, and smack too strongly of Judaism, indeed of a worse superstition), yet they must not be made without their parents’ consent.

Just as unlawful are oaths and other such means by which children bind themselves to do things neither commanded nor forbidden, which their parents are not willing for them to do. What does this do but bring a snare on the consciences of children, and make it necessary for them to break one of God’s commandments? Either the third, in breaking their vow or oath, or the fifth, in disobeying their parents.
